#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193931 :tutorial/content "We can use our knowledge of fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2.\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model for $\\dfrac{3}{10}{:}$ \n\n![](images/74/7441921087fa0748dc0de6843c45c93fd9c492bade22fc8ae31192fd9c943016.png)\n\n \n\nMultiplying $\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{10}$ by $\\color{blue}2$ means that we need to repeat the fraction $\\color{blue}2$ times.\n\n![](images/88/88ba96e731a6242aecea0a6b059933e128efdf6bbb52f37a0a73e57a004ab96c.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we count the number of shaded pieces. \n\nThere are ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2 = \\dfrac{6}{10}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bfe48a90-960a-5004-8a63-a58ea72103ff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8518 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193932 :tutorial/content "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times 4\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent $\\dfrac{2}{9}.$\n\n![](images/d3/d3c46b3452b6d5d22a30a9949800d7a69bdacf4135615627881c9dc69c0a5513.png)\n\n \n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $4$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $4.$ \n\nOur model has $2$ shaded pieces. So, the resulting model must have $2 \\times 4 = 8$ shaded pieces:\n\n![](images/b9/b99a75597e9e06cdd0c267b291ae0d671d670f7a3fc4b9af4555d9b217a0b4ed.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\\times 4 = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dbb3bf95-1b0b-52ea-b1ff-7a7a7c5d11f6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8524 :tutorial/title "A Faster Method for Multiplying Fractions by Whole Numbers"}]}
